<!-- 9 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-ecc">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">09</span><h2>Eccentric Loading</h2></div>

  <p>A column carrying moment, or sitting off-centre, produces a resultant that does not act at the middle of the footing. Meyerhof's <strong>effective area</strong> method handles this with one elegant idea.</p>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="vcard v-vio">
      <span class="vtag">Effective dimensions</span>
      <div class="eq">\[ e = \frac&#123;M&#125;&#123;Q&#125;, \qquad B' = B - 2e \]</div>
      <p class="small">Pretend the footing is only as wide as the part symmetric about the load. Compute capacity using B' in place of B, then multiply by the effective area A' = B'L.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="vcard v-amb">
      <span class="vtag">Why it works</span>
      <p>The eccentric load is equivalent to a centric load on a narrower footing. Anything beyond that symmetric strip is not helping to resist the load &mdash; it is dead weight sitting on soil that is barely stressed. Same middle-third logic as the retaining wall chapter: keep e &le; B/6 and the whole base stays in compression.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="why-matters">
    <span>&#9889;</span>
    <div><strong>Note the double penalty.</strong> Reducing B to B' shrinks the area <em>and</em> shrinks the 0.5&gamma;BN<sub>&gamma;</sub> term, so a modest eccentricity costs disproportionately more capacity than you would expect. This is why designers work hard to centre footings under their loads.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 10 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-examples">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">10</span><h2>Worked Examples</h2></div>

  <div class="example">
    <div class="ex-h"><span>Example 1</span><h4>Square Footing on Dry Sand</h4></div>
    <p class="prob"><strong>Problem:</strong> A 2 m &times; 2 m square footing is founded at D<sub>f</sub> = 1.5 m in sand with &phi;' = 30&deg;, c' = 0, &gamma; = 18 kN/m&sup3;. The water table is very deep. Find the allowable column load with FS = 3.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><b>1</b><div><strong>Bearing capacity factors</strong> at &phi;' = 30&deg;: \( N_q = e^&#123;\pi\tan30&#125;\tan^2(60) = 18.40 \), \( N_c = (18.40-1)\cot30 = 30.14 \), \( N_\gamma = 2(19.40)\tan30 = 22.40 \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>2</b><div><strong>Surcharge:</strong> \( q = \gamma D_f = 18(1.5) = 27.0\text&#123;kPa&#125; \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>3</b><div><strong>Shape factors</strong> (B/L = 1): \( F_&#123;qs&#125; = 1 + \tan30 = 1.577 \), \( F_&#123;\gamma s&#125; = 1 - 0.4 = 0.600 \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>4</b><div><strong>Depth factors</strong> (D<sub>f</sub>/B = 0.75): \( F_&#123;qd&#125; = 1 + 2\tan30(1-\sin30)^2(0.75) = 1.217 \), \( F_&#123;\gamma d&#125; = 1 \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>5</b><div><strong>Ultimate capacity</strong> (c' = 0, so the first term vanishes):
        \( q_u = 27.0(18.40)(1.577)(1.217) + 0.5(18)(2)(22.40)(0.600)(1) \)
        \( = 953.5 + 241.9 = 1195\text&#123;kPa&#125; \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>6</b><div><strong>Net and allowable:</strong> \( q_&#123;u(net)&#125; = 1195 - 27 = 1168\text&#123;kPa&#125; \); \( q_&#123;all&#125; = 1168/3 = 389\text&#123;kPa&#125; \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>7</b><div><strong>Column load:</strong> \( Q_&#123;all&#125; = 389 \times 2 \times 2 = 1558\text&#123;kN&#125; \)</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> q<sub>all(net)</sub> = 389 kPa, Q<sub>all</sub> &asymp; 1560 kN. Note the surcharge term supplies 80% of the capacity &mdash; embedment, not width, is doing most of the work here.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="example">
    <div class="ex-h"><span>Example 2</span><h4>The Same Footing with a High Water Table</h4></div>
    <p class="prob"><strong>Problem:</strong> Repeat Example 1 with the water table at the ground surface. &gamma;<sub>sat</sub> = 20 kN/m&sup3;.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><b>1</b><div><strong>Case I</strong> (water above the footing base, D&#8321; = 0): \( \gamma' = 20 - 9.81 = 10.19\text&#123;kN/m&#125;^3 \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>2</b><div><strong>Effective surcharge:</strong> \( q = \gamma' D_f = 10.19(1.5) = 15.3\text&#123;kPa&#125; \) &mdash; down from 27.0</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>3</b><div><strong>Self-weight term uses &gamma;' too:</strong> \( 0.5(10.19)(2)(22.40)(0.600) = 137.0\text&#123;kPa&#125; \) &mdash; down from 241.9</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>4</b><div>\( q_u = 15.3(18.40)(1.577)(1.217) + 137.0 = 540.1 + 137.0 = 677\text&#123;kPa&#125; \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>5</b><div>\( q_&#123;all&#125; = (677-15.3)/3 = 220.5\text&#123;kPa&#125; \), so \( Q_&#123;all&#125; = 220.5(4) = 882\text&#123;kN&#125; \)</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> Capacity falls from 1560 kN to 882 kN &mdash; a <strong>43% loss</strong> from groundwater alone, with no change to the soil or the footing. This is why the design water table is one of the most consequential decisions in the report.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="example">
    <div class="ex-h"><span>Example 3</span><h4>Eccentric Load</h4></div>
    <p class="prob"><strong>Problem:</strong> The footing of Example 1 also carries a moment M = 300 kN&middot;m alongside a vertical load Q = 1000 kN. Check whether it is still adequate.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><b>1</b><div><strong>Eccentricity:</strong> \( e = M/Q = 300/1000 = 0.30\text&#123;m&#125; \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>2</b><div><strong>Middle-third check:</strong> \( B/6 = 2/6 = 0.333\text&#123;m&#125; > 0.30 \) &mdash; the whole base stays in compression &check;</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>3</b><div><strong>Effective width:</strong> \( B' = B - 2e = 2 - 0.60 = 1.40\text&#123;m&#125; \), so \( A' = 1.40 \times 2 = 2.80\text&#123;m&#125;^2 \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>4</b><div><strong>Recompute with B' = 1.40 m.</strong> Shape factors use B'/L = 0.70: \( F_&#123;qs&#125; = 1+0.70\tan30 = 1.404 \), \( F_&#123;\gamma s&#125; = 1-0.4(0.70) = 0.720 \). Depth factors keep the <em>actual</em> B: \( F_&#123;qd&#125; = 1.217 \). The self-weight term uses B':
        \( q_u = 27(18.40)(1.404)(1.217) + 0.5(18)(1.40)(22.40)(0.720) = 848.6 + 203.2 = 1052\text&#123;kPa&#125; \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>5</b><div><strong>Allowable:</strong> \( q_&#123;all&#125; = (1052-27)/3 = 342\text&#123;kPa&#125; \), so \( Q_&#123;all&#125; = 342 \times 2.80 = 957\text&#123;kN&#125; \) against an applied 1000 kN &mdash; <strong>inadequate</strong>.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> Q<sub>all</sub> &asymp; 957 kN &lt; 1000 kN applied &mdash; the footing fails, despite passing the middle-third check. Enlarge the footing or reduce the eccentricity. Note it carried 1560 kN centrically: a 0.3 m eccentricity cost 39% of the capacity. (Shape factors take B', depth factors take the real B &mdash; a standard convention worth getting right.)</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 11 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-quiz">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">11</span><h2>Quick Reference &amp; Quick Check</h2></div>

  <div class="qref">
    <div><span>Terzaghi, strip</span><span>c'N<sub>c</sub> + qN<sub>q</sub> + 0.5&gamma;BN<sub>&gamma;</sub></span></div>
    <div><span>Terzaghi, square</span><span>1.3c'N<sub>c</sub> + qN<sub>q</sub> + 0.4&gamma;BN<sub>&gamma;</sub></span></div>
    <div><span>N<sub>q</sub></span><span>e^(&pi;tan&phi;')tan&sup2;(45+&phi;'/2)</span></div>
    <div><span>N<sub>c</sub> / N<sub>&gamma;</sub></span><span>(N<sub>q</sub>&minus;1)cot&phi;' / 2(N<sub>q</sub>+1)tan&phi;'</span></div>
    <div><span>N<sub>c</sub> at &phi;' = 0</span><span>5.14</span></div>
    <div><span>Surcharge</span><span>q = &gamma;D<sub>f</sub></span></div>
    <div><span>Net ultimate</span><span>q<sub>u(net)</sub> = q<sub>u</sub> &minus; q</span></div>
    <div><span>Allowable</span><span>(q<sub>u</sub> &minus; q)/FS, FS = 3</span></div>
    <div><span>Local shear correction</span><span>c* = ⅔c, tan&phi;* = ⅔tan&phi;</span></div>
    <div><span>Eccentric loading</span><span>B' = B &minus; 2e, A' = B'L</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz" id="quiz">
    <div class="q" data-a="1">
      <p>1. A footing on soft clay passes the bearing capacity check with FS = 3.5. Is the design complete?</p>
      <button data-v="0">Yes — FS exceeds the required 3</button>
      <button data-v="1">No — settlement must be checked separately, and on soft clay it often governs</button>
      <button data-v="2">No — FS must be at least 5 for clay</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q" data-a="2">
      <p>2. Why does raising the water table to the ground surface roughly halve the bearing capacity?</p>
      <button data-v="0">Water lubricates the soil grains, lowering &phi;'</button>
      <button data-v="1">The footing floats</button>
      <button data-v="2">Both surcharge and self-weight terms use effective unit weight, which is about half the moist value</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q" data-a="0">
      <p>3. Which failure mode gives the least warning before it occurs?</p>
      <button data-v="0">Punching shear — no slip surface reaches the ground surface</button>
      <button data-v="1">General shear — it is sudden and catastrophic</button>
      <button data-v="2">Local shear — the load-settlement curve has no peak</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q" data-a="1">
      <p>4. Terzaghi's equation is applied directly to a footing on loose sand. What is the problem?</p>
      <button data-v="0">Nothing — the equation covers all soils</button>
      <button data-v="1">It assumes general shear; loose sand punches, so c and tan&phi; must be reduced to ⅔</button>
      <button data-v="2">Loose sand has no bearing capacity at all</button>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div id="quiz-score" class="quiz-score"></div>
</section>

